In prior work, the author has characterized the real numbers $a,b,c$ and $1\leq p,q,r<\infty $ such that the weighted Sobolev space $W_{\{a,b\}}^{(q,p)}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}):=\{u\in L_{loc}^{1}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}):|x|^{ rac{a}{q}}u\in L^{q}(R^{N}),|x|^{ rac{b}{p}}
abla u\in (L^{p}(R^{N}))^{N}\}$ is continuously embedded into $L^{r}(R^{N};|x|^{c}dx) :=\{u\in L_{loc}^{1}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}):|x|^{ rac{c}{r}}u\in L^{r}(R^{N})\}$. This paper discusses the embedding question for $W_{\{a,b\}}^{(\infty, p)}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}):=\{u\in L_{loc}^{1}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}):|x|^{a}u\in L^{\infty}(R^{N}),|x|^{ rac{b}{p}}
abla u\in (L^{p}(R^{N}))^{N}\}$, which is not the space obtained by the formal substitution $q=\infty$ in the previous definition of $W_{\{a,b\}}^{(q,p)}(R^{N} ackslash \{0}),$ unless $a=0$. The corresponding embedding theorem identifies all the real numbers $a,b,c$ and $1\leq p,r<\infty $ such that $W_{\{a,b\}}^{(\infty, p)}(R^{N}  ackslash \{0})$ is continuously embedded in $L^{r}(R^{N};|x|^{c}dx)$. A notable feature is that such embeddings exist only when $a
eq 0$ and, in particular, have no analog in the unweighted setting. It is also shown that the embeddings are always accounted for by multiplicative rather than just additive norm inequalities. These inequalities are natural extensions of the Caffarelli-Kohn-Nirenberg inequalities which, in their known form, are restricted to functions of $C_{0}^{\infty}(R^{N})$ and do not incorporate supremum norms.

Let $λ(n)$ denote the Liouville function. Complementary to the prime number theorem, Chowla conjectured that \vspace{1mm} 
oindent { f Conjecture (Chowla).} {\em  egin{equation} \label{a.1} \sum_{n\le x} λ(f(n)) =o(x) \end{equation} for any polynomial $f(x)$ with integer coefficients which is not of form $bg(x)^2$. } \vspace{1mm} 
oindent The prime number theorem is equivalent to \eqref{a.1} when $f(x)=x$. Chowla's conjecture is proved for linear functions but for the degree greater than 1, the conjecture seems to be extremely hard and still remains wide open. One can consider a weaker form of Chowla's conjecture, namely, \vspace{1mm} 
oindent { f Conjecture 1 (Cassaigne, et al).} {\em If $f(x) \in \Z [x]$ and is not in the form of $bg^2(x)$ for some $g(x)\in \Z[x]$, then $λ(f(n))$ changes sign infinitely often.} Clearly, Chowla's conjecture implies Conjecture 1. Although it is weaker, Conjecture 1 is still wide open for polynomials of degree $>1$. In this article, we study Conjecture 1 for the quadratic polynomials. One of our main theorems is { f Theorem 1.} {\em Let $f(x) = ax^2+bx +c $ with $a>0$ and $l$ be a positive integer such that $al$ is not a perfect square. Then if the equation $f(n)=lm^2 $ has one solution $(n_0,m_0) \in \Z^2$, then it has infinitely many positive solutions $(n,m) \in \N^2$.} As a direct consequence of Theorem 1, we prove some partial results of Conjecture 1 for quadratic polynomials are also proved by using Theorem 1.
